<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Axioms $(i)$ and $(ii)$ hold by assumption. To show that coproducts in $\calX $ are disjoint, let us consider an arbitrary pair of objects $X, Y \in \calX $, and let $\emptyset $ denote an initial object of $\calX $. Let $f: \emptyset \rightarrow X$ be a morphism (unique up to homotopy, since $\emptyset $ is initial). Since colimits in $\calX $ are universal, $f$ is a monomorphism (Lemma <a href="/tag/46CE93" data-tag="46CE93">6.4.4.8</a>) and therefore belongs to $\calO _{\calX }^{n}$, since $n \geq 1$. We observe that $\id _{\emptyset }$ is an initial object of $\calO _{\calX }$, so we can form a pushout diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { \id _{\emptyset } \ar [r]^{\alpha } \ar [d]^{\beta } &  \id _{Y} \ar [d]^{\beta '} \\ f \ar [r]^{\alpha '} &  g } \]
</div>
    <p>
       in $\calO ^{n}_{\calX }$. It is clear that $\alpha $ is a Cartesian transformation, and Lemma <a href="/tag/0FD9C4" data-tag="0FD9C4">6.1.3.6</a> implies that $\beta $ is Cartesian as well. Invoking Theorem <a href="/tag/A242E8" data-tag="A242E8">6.4.4.5</a>, we deduce that $\alpha '$ is a Cartesian transformation. But $\alpha '$ can be identified with a pushout diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { \emptyset \ar [r] \ar [d] &  Y \ar [d] \\ X \ar [r] &  X \amalg Y. } \]
</div>
    <p>
       This proves $(iii)$. 
    <p>
      Now suppose that $U_{\bullet }$ is an $n$-efficient groupoid object of $\calX $; we wish to prove that $U_{\bullet }$ is effective. Let $\overline{U}_{\bullet } : \Nerve (\cDelta _{+})^{op} \rightarrow \calX $ be a colimit of $U_{\bullet }$. Let $U'_{\bullet }: \Nerve (\cDelta _{+})^{op} \rightarrow \calX $ be the result of composing $\overline{U}_{\bullet }$ with the shift functor 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \cDelta _{+} \rightarrow \cDelta _{+} \]
</div>
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  J \mapsto J \amalg \{  \infty \} . \]
</div>
    <p>
       (In other words, $U'_{\bullet }$ is the shifted simplicial object given by $U'_{n} = U_{n+1}$.) Lemma <a href="/tag/F41DC5" data-tag="F41DC5">6.1.3.17</a> asserts that $U'_{\bullet }$ is a colimit diagram in $\calX $. We have a transformation $\overline{\alpha }: U'_{\bullet } \rightarrow \overline{U}_{\bullet }$. Let $\overline{V}_{\bullet }$ denote the constant augmented simplicial object of $\calX $ taking the value $U_0$, so that we have a natural transformation $\overline{\beta }: U'_{\bullet } \rightarrow \overline{V}_{\bullet }$. Let $\overline{W}_{\bullet }$ denote a product of $\overline{U}_{\bullet }$ and $\overline{V}_{\bullet }$ in the $\infty $-category $\calX _{\Delta _{+}}$ of augmented simplicial objects, and let $\overline{\gamma }: U'_{\bullet } \rightarrow \overline{W}_{\bullet }$ be the induced map. We observe that for each $n \geq 0$, the map $\overline{\gamma }(\Delta ^ n): U_{n+1} \rightarrow \overline{W}_ n$ is a pullback of $U_1 \rightarrow U_0 \times U_0$, and therefore $(n-2)$-truncated (since $U_{\bullet }$ is assumed to be $n$-efficient). Since $U_{\bullet }$ is a groupoid, we conclude that $\gamma = \overline{\gamma } | \Nerve (\cDelta )^{op}$ is a Cartesian transformation. Invoking Theorem <a href="/tag/A242E8" data-tag="A242E8">6.4.4.5</a>, we deduce that $\overline{\gamma }$ is also a Cartesian transformation, so that the diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { U_1 \ar [r] \ar [d] &  U_0 \ar [d] \\ \overline{W}_0 \ar [r] &  U_0 \times \overline{W}_{-1} } \]
</div>
    <p>
       is Cartesian. Combining this with the Cartesian diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { \overline{W}_0 \ar [r] \ar [d] &  \overline{W}_{-1} \ar [d] \\ U_0 \ar [r] &  \overline{U}_{-1} } \]
</div>
    <p>
       we deduce that $U$ is effective, as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>